NAME="Florian's Linux From Scratch"
ID=flfs
# PRETTY_NAME is what almost everything actually displays, and its absence was visible
# in two places at once: agetty printed the bare fallback "Linux" above the login
# prompt, and flfsfetch fell back to NAME. os-release(5) says consumers should use
# PRETTY_NAME and only fall back to NAME, so shipping just NAME meant relying on the
# fallback everywhere.
PRETTY_NAME="Florian's Linux From Scratch"
HOME_URL="https://github.com/fwilhe2/glowing-octo-robot"
ANSI_COLOR="0;36"
# Deliberately no VERSION, VERSION_ID or BUILD_ID *here*: they are not properties of the
# repository, they are properties of a build, and os-release(5) treats all three as
# optional. An invented value would be worse than an absent one — consumers can tell "not
# versioned" from "version 1", but not from a number that means nothing.
#
# image/build-rootfs.sh appends them when a build actually has them, from FLFS_VERSION (a
# release, i.e. a CalVer date cut by the release workflow) and FLFS_BUILD (the commit,
# which every CI build has). It also folds the version into PRETTY_NAME, which is the
# field the paragraph above says everything displays. A build with neither — a local
# `podman run`, or a PR — produces this file unchanged, which is the honest answer for
# something that is not a release of anything.
